  "text": "burseuserponse\n\nburse User Response:\n\nburse We will start by finding the center of the ellipse and the hyperbola.\n\nburse For the ellipse defined by $\\frac{x^2}{16} + \\frac{y^2}{9} = 1$, the center is $(0,0)$. \n\nburse For the hyperbola defined by $\\frac{x^2}{9} - \\frac{y^2}{4} = 1$, the center is also $(0,0)$. \n\nburse Therefore, the line that passes through the center of the ellipse and hyperbola is $x = 0$.\n\nburse This line is tangent to the ellipse at $(4,0)$ and tangent to the hyperbola at $(3,0)$.\n\nburse The equation of the line is $x = 0$.\n\nburse Simplifying the equation, we get $0x + 1y = 0$. \n\nburse Therefore, the values of $p, q, r$ are $0, 1, 0$.\n\nburse The sum of $p, q, r$ is $0 + 1 + 0 = 1$.\n\nburse Therefore, the final answer is $p + q + r = \\boxed{1}$.\n\nburse",
